WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:03.629
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:08.189
Following the success of Maleficent and Cinderella,
Disney continues the live-action make-over

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.590
of their classic animations with The Jungle
Book.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:16.039
Drawing on both Rudyard Kipling's original
stories and Disney's 1967 animated feature,

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:19.070
this new Jungle Book is an absolute feast
for the eyes.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:24.400
The film almost seamlessly blends live-action
performance with absolutely astounding CG

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.730
locations and amazingly photo-real animals.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:31.550
You'll almost not believe that you're watching
mainly computer-generated images, so convincing

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:37.220
is the VFX work by WETA, MPC and Digital Domain,
as well as Animation Supervisor Andrew R Jones

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.220
and his crew.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.020
When I reviewed Pixar's The Good Dinosaur
last year, I said that the stunning realism

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:45.539
of the landscapes made you feel as if you
could reach out and touch them.

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:50.239
And the same is true of The Jungle Book...
it's all so incredibly life-like: from the

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:55.800
beautiful waterfall to the pouring rain, ravines
oozing mud, grass swaying gently in the breeze,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.829
and the movement of leaves on the trees.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:00.040
It really is a sight to behold.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:05.880
And I saw this movie in 3D, which made it
feel like an even more immersive experience.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.140
At the heart of The Jungle Book is newcomer
Neel Sethi as the movie's only human character,

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.140
Mowgli.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.980
Sethi does a great job, especially considering
not only has he never acted professionally,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.740
but he also had to act with CG characters
in this film.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.579
He really does respond beautifully well to
whatever's needed of him, whether in the more

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:26.810
action-filled scenes, the quieter sections,
or the more humorous moments.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:32.229
The one place he's not as skilled is the singing
department, but that's not such as big deal

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:34.030
as he doesn't have so much to do there.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:38.189
As for the movie's starry voice cast, it's
pretty superb all round.

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:42.630
Ben Kingsley is perfect as the black panther
Bagheera, bringing a wonderful gentle but

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:47.159
commanding quality to the part, a performance
which I rate as highly as Sebastian Cabot's

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.180
original take on the character in the 1967
animation.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:55.730
Idris Elba takes a completely different tack
to the fabulous George Sanders' sleekly-voiced

00:01:55.730 --> 00:02:01.000
villain Shere Khan, giving a much more overtly
menacing tone to the tiger who's desperate

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.390
to get his claws on man-cub Mowgli.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.780
In this version of the story, Shere Khan gets
much more screen-time, and I liked Elba's

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.510
suitably powerful and steely portrayal, which
nicely fits this fresh interpretation of the

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.510
tiger.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.360
And I love the way the sound guys seem to
have sometimes mixed in real tiger growls

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.610
underneath Elba's wonderfully rich, resounding
voice.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:25.580
Of course, if you know the original animation
well, you're not going to forget Phil Harris's

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:30.480
brilliant John-Wayne-style Baloo voice in
a hurry, but Bill Murray really is very humourous

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.110
his role as everyone's favourite lazy bones
bear, and his take on the character definitely

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.110
works for me.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.450
Oh, and we get to hear a little of his rendition
of the fantastic Bare Necessities too, though

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.760
I would definitely have loved more!

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.370
Taking on jazzman Louis Prima's role of King
Louie, Christopher Walken puts his distinctive

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:51.870
voice to good use playing the now King Kong-sized
orangutan as a mob boss.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.341
Although Walken sings a couple of verses of
'I Wanna Be Like You' with some new lyrics,

00:02:55.341 --> 00:03:00.760
the way the song's introduced doesn't feel
as natural a fit in this version of the story

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.750
as it was in the animated movie.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:07.820
Lupita Nyong'o puts in a tender turn as Mowgli's
wolf-mother, Raksha, alongside the always-welcome

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.980
presence of Giancarlo Esposito as his adoptive
wolf father, Akela.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.910
Scarlett Johansson brings a fittingly mesmerising
lilt to the python Kaa, and underneath her

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.310
voice the sound department has added a little
snake-like hiss and the occasional sound of

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.870
a snake's tongue flicking, which really adds
to the whole snakey effect!

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.250
It's a real shame she's only on screen for
a short time, and, given she's a singer as

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:33.310
well as an actress, I would have loved to
have heard her perform Trust In Me during

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.310
the actual movie.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:39.050
By the way, do look out for a prickly little
comic cameo from the late Garry Shandling

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.390
in what's sadly his final role.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:45.410
The Jungle Book kicks off at a breathless
pace and screenwriter Justin Marks ensures

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:49.541
there's plenty of adventure and humour alongside
darker and more emotional moments.

00:03:49.541 --> 00:03:53.810
And there's one particular part of the movie,
which I won't spoil for you here, that's actually

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:57.670
handled in a much more interesting way than
in the original animation.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:01.810
There's also a bunch of Easter Eggs and call-backs
to the original animated movie, and I'll have

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:05.980
a video on that up soon, so don't forget to
check it out when it's live – there'll be

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:06.980
a link here!

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:11.370
Director Jon Favreau, who gave us the much-loved
modern Christmas classic Elf and successfully

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:15.780
kicked off the Marvel movie juggernaut with
Iron Man, clearly hasn't lost his touch.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.650
Although one action scene cut have done with
a little cutting, Favreau, along with Matrix

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:24.310
cinematographer Bill Pope, keeps up the movie's
energy through most of its 105-minute run

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.370
time.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.660
And composer John Debney has written a rich
original score, which also nicely brings in

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.080
themes from the 1967 movie.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.810
By the way, if you're someone who normally
bolts straight out of the cinema as soon as

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.530
the film's finished, don't do that for this
movie.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:42.220
Make sure you stay through the credits as
they're brilliantly imaginative and you'll

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.750
get to hear three great songs, two of which
you hear snippets from in the film, and a

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.820
third one that you can only hear in the credits.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:54.550
So, to sum up, The Jungle Book is a gobsmackingly
gorgeous movie which combines unbelievably

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:59.590
brilliant technological wizardry, a fantastic
cast, and enough nods to the animated classic

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.640
it updates, even if it's not quite everything
you'd want it to be in the song-and-dance

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:04.640
department.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.430
Oh, and it's a real treat to watch in 3D.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.750
The Jungle Books gets 4 out of 5 Flicks.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.320
Now, tell me what do you think about The Jungle
Book?

